        drivingExperience: "The 115hp N45-equipped 116i provides a surprisingly analog and balanced driving experience, defined by the brilliant rear-wheel-drive E87 chassis. While the 1.6-liter engine is undeniably slow and requires constant revving to keep up with modern traffic, the heavy, hydraulic power steering and near 50:50 weight distribution make it incredibly engaging to drive on twisty roads. The lack of power means you can drive it at ten-tenths without breaking the speed limit. Mated to a slick-shifting Getrag manual gearbox, it feels like a momentum car—rewarding drivers who carry speed through corners rather than relying on straight-line acceleration.",
        mechanicVerdict: "The N45 1.6L is arguably one of the most frustrating 4-cylinder engines BMW produced in this era. While it avoids the highly complex Valvetronic system of the N46, it suffers from a catastrophically flawed timing chain tensioner design. If you buy this car and the tensioner has not been updated, it is a ticking time bomb that will jump teeth and destroy the valvetrain. Furthermore, you will constantly battle severe oil leaks from the filter housing and valve cover, as well as failing VANOS solenoids. If you proactively fix the timing tensioner and address the oil leaks, the block itself is durable, but the maintenance costs heavily outweigh the slow performance it offers.",
        repairCostEstimate: "$800 - $1,500 / year (While basic maintenance is cheap, the N45 requires expensive, labor-intensive repairs to survive. Replacing the timing chain kit, fixing the inevitable oil filter housing and valve cover leaks, and replacing clogged VANOS solenoids will easily run into the thousands of euros. If the timing chain snaps, the engine is effectively totaled)."
